WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:01.593
- You look beautiful!

00:00:01.593 --> 00:00:02.580
- I mean...

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.440
- It's like we called
each other (laughing).

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.520
- Yeah, we don't even have
each other's phone number,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.728
but, like, telepathically we're like...

00:00:08.728 --> 00:00:09.561
- Somehow.

00:00:09.561 --> 00:00:11.560
I was just that one outside
your house just watching

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.393
what you were gonna wear.

00:00:12.393 --> 00:00:13.339
- (laughing) Well,
- How are you?

00:00:13.339 --> 00:00:16.560
Almost everything is leopard
so you did look in my house.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.960
- I know, I like an animal print,

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.878
I'm not afraid of it.

00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:20.880
And especially when
they're silky and soft.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.919
- I know, that looks amazing on you.

00:00:21.919 --> 00:00:23.644
- But then you find yourself
touching yourself like this

00:00:23.644 --> 00:00:24.701
and people probably think that's weird.

00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:25.680
- I know, they're like,
"What are they doing?"

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.709
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:26.709 --> 00:00:27.542
(laughing)

00:00:27.542 --> 00:00:28.730
How is your family doing?

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.240
They're doing amazing.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.240
It's so fun to have a baby,
and she's about to be one.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.200
And then the other kids are
just getting so old now,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.910
and they just have a mind of their own.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:44.430
- [Kelly] Are they too cool yet?

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:47.842
- I mean, they have better
ideas than any of us.

00:00:47.842 --> 00:00:48.977
- Yeah, that's my kids, yeah.

00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:52.190
- Like, I have to ask Maxwell
if I look cute or not,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.257
because I feel like she
would just look at me like,

00:00:54.257 --> 00:00:56.296
"Mm, why are you wearing
that?", you know (laughing)?

00:00:56.296 --> 00:00:57.129
(audience laughing)

00:00:57.129 --> 00:00:59.460
- Oh my God, I think my
kids sense that they need

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.760
to be positive with me or something,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.593
'cause they're always like
- Exactly,

00:01:01.593 --> 00:01:02.590
"You look beautiful, Mommy."

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.814
and I look horrible, and
I'm like, "Thank you, liar."

00:01:05.814 --> 00:01:07.390
(laughing)

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:09.623
Do your kids like it when you sing?

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:11.931
- Yes, they do.

00:01:11.931 --> 00:01:14.490
(stuttering)

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.740
With them growing up,
they didn't see me perform

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.070
until I got on stage with Willy Nelson

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.400
right before I found out
I was pregnant with Birdy.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:25.233
- [Kelly] Oh, wow.

00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:28.890
- Yeah so, I mean I sang to them, yes.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:33.623
But with my daughter,
my first born, Maxwell,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.607
when I was singing lullaby's to her,

00:01:36.607 --> 00:01:41.607
I was like, "This is not
gonna make her stop crying."

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.760
'Cause it's like, "When the bow breaks,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.900
the cradle will fall,
and down will come baby,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.930
cradle and all."

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:47.919
- Everyone dies, yeah.

00:01:47.919 --> 00:01:49.450
- Everybody dies!

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:51.090
And so I just sang about America,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.296
I sang "The Star Spangled Banner",

00:01:52.296 --> 00:01:54.140
(laughing hysterically)

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:56.897
and I was like, "Okay,
she's crying so loud,

00:01:56.897 --> 00:02:00.310
"and I have some pipes
and a set of lungs."

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.420
I can be louder than her,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.900
so I just belted out "The
Star Spangled Banner".

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.768
And now she sings it in the
shower at the top of her lungs.

00:02:06.768 --> 00:02:07.750
(laughing)

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.180
And she does such a great job,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.270
so I take credit for that.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:11.103
That was my lullaby.

00:02:11.103 --> 00:02:11.936
- [Kelly] Such a credit for that.

00:02:11.936 --> 00:02:12.769
(audience applauding)

00:02:12.769 --> 00:02:13.602
(laughing)

00:02:13.602 --> 00:02:14.660
"That was me."

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.830
Your new book, I mean, as a fellow Texan,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:18.767
there could not be a
better title for a Texan.

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:19.680
(laughing)

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.240
Your memoir is called "Open Book".

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.073
- Yes.

00:02:22.073 --> 00:02:24.020
- And is this, like, hard that you,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.087
'cause normally I would ask people,

00:02:25.087 --> 00:02:26.590
"Is it hard to share personal stories?",

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:27.970
but I feel like we might be similar,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.220
and it's almost cathartic.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.053
- Yeah

00:02:30.053 --> 00:02:31.030
- [Kelly] You're such
a, you have no filter.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:31.863
- I have no filter.

00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:32.870
- Which I don't either.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:33.879
- [Jessica] I've never had a filter.

00:02:33.879 --> 00:02:34.712
- Me neither.

00:02:34.712 --> 00:02:37.330
- I think it's very important
just to be yourself.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:38.410
And that's what's authentic,

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:39.301
and that's what people love about you.

00:02:39.301 --> 00:02:40.259
- Yeah.

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:41.092
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:02:41.092 --> 00:02:41.925
- I think that's what
people love about you,

00:02:41.925 --> 00:02:43.170
is that you're very honest.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.003
- I'm very honest.

00:02:44.003 --> 00:02:44.980
- Especially in this book.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.750
You opened up about some
pretty personal stuff

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:47.583
that's hard to get through.

00:02:47.583 --> 00:02:49.220
And most people, I feel like,

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:50.730
would be afraid to say
that in the public eye.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.210
But I think it's so brave of you,

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.500
and so cool of you, and strong of you,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.910
because, one, when you address that,

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.750
then you know you're
actually getting past it.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:02:59.173
- Yeah, I mean it definitely,

00:02:59.173 --> 00:03:02.230
my openness holds me accountable.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:07.230
And for me, when I decided to get sober,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.720
I stopped drinking and I just
had so much clarity, that,

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.850
with my openness, it was
just like I started learning

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.530
so much about myself
and why I had to drink

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.239
to try and escape,
- Escape.

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:24.320
And try and just feel just normal.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.640
But that was, I thought it
was helping with my anxiety,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.440
and it was actually making it worse.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:30.747
- Yeah, 'cause people will say that like,

00:03:30.747 --> 00:03:32.400
"Oh, it's calming.", they
say that all the time.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.460
- It's absolutely not calming.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.610
- I know, but I just think
it was really brave of you

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.353
to come out and say that.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.330
Has it been rewarding, getting it out?

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.060
- It has, it's been unbelievable.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.070
I've been on this book tour,

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:49.090
and every city that I'm
going into it's like,

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:51.238
these people that come
up to me, they're like,

00:03:51.238 --> 00:03:55.618
"I'm gonna be sober,
I wanna tell you first

00:03:55.618 --> 00:03:58.960
"before I tell my family and my parents."

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.139
- [Kelly] Yeah, it's connection,

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:03.030
- It's a really deep connection.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:06.500
I've had mothers come up
to me and thanking me.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.990
Like, they're not afraid of therapy.

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:09.823
For some reason...

00:04:09.823 --> 00:04:10.656
- Why are people afraid of that?

00:04:10.656 --> 00:04:11.489
- People are so afraid

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:12.322
I'm in therapy, I love therapy.

00:04:12.322 --> 00:04:13.630
I know, I love therapy.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.260
It's like no judgment, you know?

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.650
- Yeah, you can say whatever you want

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:17.483
and they're not in your circle.

00:04:17.483 --> 00:04:18.316
- [Jessica] I know.

00:04:18.316 --> 00:04:19.149
- You will not be judged.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:19.982
- [Jessica] You will not be judged.

00:04:19.982 --> 00:04:20.815
- And even if you are, you don't know,

00:04:20.815 --> 00:04:21.648
you're paying them, it's fine.

00:04:21.648 --> 00:04:22.863
- Yeah, exactly, exactly.

00:04:22.863 --> 00:04:25.000
They're not your friends.
- I'm paying you to listen.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:26.560
My therapist, at the very beginning,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.810
I was like, "Oh my Gosh, I
feel like we're best friends."

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.040
And she goes, "I wanna make it very clear

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:31.873
that we're not friends."

00:04:31.873 --> 00:04:33.710
(audience laughing)

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.191
(laughing)

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:37.190
And I was like, "I don't know if anybody

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:39.238
has ever said that to me."

00:04:39.238 --> 00:04:40.640
- I wanna make this very clear,

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.434
I don't wanna be your friend.
- We're not friends.

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:44.832
Yeah, we're not friends.

00:04:44.832 --> 00:04:46.136
- (laughing) I love your therapist.

00:04:46.136 --> 00:04:47.523
(laughing)

00:04:47.523 --> 00:04:49.723
I've never asked mine,
but now I'm going to.

00:04:51.205 --> 00:04:54.280
- Then it actually made me
wanna make her be my friend,

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.900
so I was gonna be open more.

00:04:58.605 --> 00:04:59.560
- [Kelly] You're gonna want this.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.828
- I mean, you're gonna
want me to be your daughter

00:05:00.828 --> 00:05:01.667
by the end of this.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:03.460
(laughing)

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.520
- It felt as if it's a challenge.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.490
That's so Texan.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.280
Okay, well, Jessica's book has been out

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:08.700
for a little over a month.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.930
It even hit number one
on "The New York Times"

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.115
best sellers, which is amazing.

00:05:13.115 --> 00:05:14.221
(audience applauding and cheering)

00:05:14.221 --> 00:05:15.054
- It's so exciting.

00:05:15.054 --> 00:05:17.320
- Everyone in our
audience received a copy.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.860
Let's see 'em, ya'll got your copies?

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.175
- Oh, yay!
Yay!

00:05:20.175 --> 00:05:21.587
(audience cheering)

00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:24.665
Look at that hair in that
shot, I love your hair.

00:05:24.665 --> 00:05:25.498
- You know.

00:05:25.498 --> 00:05:27.910
As Jessica just mentioned, many of them

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.160
were touched by some of
the things that she wrote.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.266
So, would you be up for
us maybe taking a couple

00:05:32.266 --> 00:05:34.630
questions?
- Absolutely yes, please.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.000
- Well, where is Ramsey?

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:36.833
Where's Ramsey?

00:05:36.833 --> 00:05:38.040
Hi!

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:39.165
- Hi Kelly, hi Jessica.

00:05:39.165 --> 00:05:39.998
- [Jessica] Hi!

00:05:39.998 --> 00:05:42.400
- What's your question?

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.850
- So Jessica, I heard that
you were in a car accident,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:48.197
and you quoted, "My
stutter started soon after,

00:05:48.197 --> 00:05:50.777
"and the doctors said it
was from the head injury.

00:05:50.777 --> 00:05:52.727
"My mom said that when
I stuttered it looked

00:05:52.727 --> 00:05:55.953
"like my brain and I were trying
to say ten things at once.

00:05:55.953 --> 00:05:57.920
"My voice just wouldn't work."

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.693
So, from this trauma, why
did you go into singing?

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:05.530
- Actually, singing was the
best way for me to communicate.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.290
- I've heard about that
with stuttering people.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:10.120
- So I got into singing, and
that was literally the way

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:15.120
that I could express myself,
and how everything was clear.

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.660
So yeah, I don't know
what caused the stutter.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.090
I mean, it coulda been
from the car accident,

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.010
something very traumatic,
- Traumatic.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:28.490
other traumatic things that
I went through as a child,

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.070
and I also have a million
thoughts in my head at all times.

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:31.903
- Me too.

00:06:31.903 --> 00:06:33.250
- Like somehow trying to balance that,

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.640
I might as well just sing through it.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.070
- I love that, I love that.

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:36.903
Great question.

00:06:36.903 --> 00:06:39.010
So next question though,
is from Ashley, right?

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:39.843
Where's Ashley?

00:06:39.843 --> 00:06:40.676
Hi, Ashley.

00:06:40.676 --> 00:06:41.509
- [Jessica] Hi, Ashley.

00:06:41.509 --> 00:06:42.342
- Hi Kelly, hi Jessica.

00:06:42.342 --> 00:06:43.290
So you're talking about being sober,

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:44.587
and you were actually just
speaking about that now,

00:06:44.587 --> 00:06:46.060
what brought you to the point

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:48.340
where you felt like you had to get sober?

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:52.810
- Well, being a mother, I
mean my kids, at the time,

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.820
were five and four.

00:06:55.820 --> 00:06:59.730
So, I grew up with no
alcohol in the house.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:03.710
And I was at a place where I was literally

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:06.975
spiraling with the alcohol.

00:07:06.975 --> 00:07:11.975
And I was missing out on
moments with my children.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.520
Then they were seeing me
and they were very confused,

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.520
and I just wanted to be
present, and have clarity,

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.510
and be a good role model for my children,

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:23.343
'cause I always wanted to be

00:07:23.343 --> 00:07:25.010
a good role model for the world,

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:28.245
so why in the world would
I be stuck in this cycle

00:07:28.245 --> 00:07:31.350
of having to wake up and have a drink

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.420
before going to one of
their school assemblies.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:35.620
It got to the point where all of my life

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:40.620
had escalated and I couldn't suppress it.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.328
And alcohol, it wasn't working,

00:07:43.328 --> 00:07:45.800
it was making just completely check out

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.488
and not be there for them.

00:07:47.488 --> 00:07:48.321
- Isn't it funny?

00:07:48.321 --> 00:07:50.090
And then you feel like, 'cause
when you're at the height

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.940
of your career, people always
think you're the happiest.

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:55.160
At the height of my career,
I was the most miserable.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:56.008
- [Jessica] Right, right, right.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:56.841
And I just wanted to quit.

00:07:56.841 --> 00:07:57.780
'Cause I was like, "This isn't worth it."

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:58.937
I was a really good waitress.

00:07:58.937 --> 00:08:00.010
(audience laughing)

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:03.210
I was like, I'm just constantly
battling just to be me,

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:04.043
which is exhausting.

00:08:04.043 --> 00:08:04.876
- [Jessica] Right. Yeah.

00:08:04.876 --> 00:08:05.709
You know?

00:08:05.709 --> 00:08:07.310
So, it's interesting that at our height

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:10.160
we can be so miserable,
- I did it for my kids,

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.180
and nobody knows.
but I also

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:15.610
realized in the process,
I was doing it for myself.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.370
I really wanted to go
back to the perspective

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:20.068
that I had when I was younger.

00:08:20.068 --> 00:08:22.304
- It's a good idea for a movie or a show.

00:08:22.304 --> 00:08:24.331
The journey of going back through that.

00:08:24.331 --> 00:08:25.164
- Yeah, it might be in the process.

00:08:25.164 --> 00:08:25.997
- Oh!

00:08:25.997 --> 00:08:26.866
See, I have good ideas.

00:08:26.866 --> 00:08:28.130
(audience applauding)

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.485
That's a great idea.

00:08:30.485 --> 00:08:32.840
Well okay, on a lighter
note, your fashion line,

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.810
everything you do is a hit.

00:08:34.810 --> 00:08:36.220
Everyone loves your shoes,
everyone loves your hair,

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:37.720
everybody loves everything you do.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.860
So, I mean, what's the
latest thing you're doing?

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:44.470
- With the fashion line,
everything is so natural.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.580
Like when I was pregnant,
we did maternity.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.664
And then after I'd had a baby I was like,

00:08:48.664 --> 00:08:52.567
"Oh, why in the world can
we not snap these buttons

00:08:52.567 --> 00:08:55.730
"in the correct way?", and
so we put a different color,

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:58.261
the button where the diaper is.

00:08:58.261 --> 00:08:59.802
- Are you talking about
where it starts to separate?

00:08:59.802 --> 00:09:00.635
- Yes!

00:09:00.635 --> 00:09:01.468
- It's so annoying.

00:09:01.468 --> 00:09:02.301
- It's so annoying.

00:09:02.301 --> 00:09:04.070
So everything has been very natural

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:07.170
and we've had such great success

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:09.510
with the Jessica Simpson Collection,

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.800
and we never had eCommerce.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.640
